      title: 'Sediments, minerals, and the environmental stakes',
      body: `**The seafloor as geological archive.** Deep-sea sediments are among the most powerful paleoclimate and paleoceanographic archives available to science. Unlike continental sediments — which are eroded, reworked, and interrupted by unconformities — the open abyssal ocean accumulates sediment continuously and slowly, producing a near-complete record from the Jurassic (when the present ocean basins opened) to today. The JOIDES Resolution, the primary vessel of the International Ocean Discovery Program (IODP), has drilled over 1,500 sites worldwide; individual cores can be correlated globally by their magnetic polarity stratigraphy, biostratigraphy, and chemical properties.

**The mineral resource problem.** The same abyssal plains that contain this geological archive are carpeted with polymetallic nodules. The Clarion-Clipperton Zone (CCZ) — a roughly 6-million-km² region of the central equatorial Pacific between Hawaii and Mexico — contains an estimated 27 billion tonnes (29.8 billion tons) of dry nodule mass by some estimates, with sufficiently high concentrations of Ni, Co, Cu, and Mn to be commercially attractive. In 2021, the total ISA-licensed exploration area in the CCZ exceeded 1.5 million km². As of 2024, no commercial mining has begun in international waters, but the race for exploration contracts has accelerated sharply as demand for battery metals has grown.

**The legal framework: UNCLOS and the ISA.** The 1982 United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) established that the mineral resources of the seabed beyond national jurisdiction (the "Area") are the "common heritage of mankind" — they cannot be appropriated by any state or private entity. The International Seabed Authority (ISA), established by UNCLOS and headquartered in Kingston, Jamaica, administers exploration and (eventually) exploitation in the Area on behalf of all humanity, with a mandate to ensure that revenues benefit developing states. As of 2024, the ISA had issued 31 exploration contracts to state-sponsored contractors from China, Japan, South Korea, Russia, France, Germany, and other states. The "two-year rule" triggered in June 2021 by Nauru — a mechanism in UNCLOS allowing a sponsoring state to force the ISA to complete mining regulations within two years — created a July 2023 deadline that passed without agreed regulations, leaving the ISA in an ongoing governance impasse. No commercial exploitation has been approved; a de facto moratorium remains in place through procedural standoff.`,
